1. Hive Router consumes federated GraphQL subgraphs over HTTP only. It cannot
   call REST. A GraphQL Mesh supergraph using @httpOperation/@resolveTo will NOT
   run on it, because only Hive Gateway executes those directives.
2. Hive Router has NO caching of any kind. Its config has no cache key.
   Response caching is open RFC graphql-hive/router#312. `storages` is S3 only.
3. Router extension points: Rust plugins (only on_http_request and
   on_graphql_params can short-circuit a request) and a language-agnostic
   coprocessor with router/graphql request+response stages.

